% ============================================================================
% friction-types — the four everyday types of friction and, for each, what it
% acts on / when it occurs, as the source states it (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of mechanics, and a rung a PHYSICS agent reasons UP
% from: the single force we call "friction" is usefully split into FOUR
% everyday types — static, sliding (kinetic), rolling, and fluid — and each type
% is distinguished by WHEN it happens or WHAT it acts on. "Static friction is
% the friction between two objects kept at rest against each other; sliding
% friction is the friction while two surfaces slide against each other; rolling
% friction is the friction on a spherical object like a wheel or a ball; fluid
% friction is the friction between the layers of a fluid in relative motion."
% Before you can reason about a block on a ramp, a braking car, a rolling wheel,
% or a swimmer, you must first know WHICH of these four regimes you are in.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? friction_context(rolling, $Context)      % spherical_object
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `friction_context(friction, context)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% context WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these four friction types (it never invents a
% context).
%
% Each of the four everyday friction types and the context (what it acts on /
% when it occurs) the source states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     friction type   context (what it acts on / when it occurs)   a beginner's cue
%     -------------   ------------------------------------------   ---------------------------
%     static          at_rest                                      two objects kept at rest
%     sliding         sliding_motion                               two surfaces sliding
%     rolling         spherical_object                             a wheel or a ball rolling
%     fluid           fluid_layers                                 layers of a fluid in motion
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a context and recall its friction type:
%
%     ? friction_context($Friction, fluid_layers)   % fluid
%
% A word that is NOT one of these four everyday friction types — `gravity`,
% `tension`, `magnetism` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a friction recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a context. That honest-abstention
% property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers
% exactly the friction->context pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `context` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% "what it acts on / when it occurs" phrase the source EXPLICITLY states for that
% friction type — never one the source does not support. (Where the everyday
% intuition differs from the source's word — the source frames rolling friction
% by the "spherical object" it acts on, and fluid friction by the "layers of
% fluid" it acts between, rather than by a "when" — the atom uses the source's
% own word, so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every friction->context
% pair below was read out of a fetched Testbook "Types of Friction" page, and
% any pair whose context could not be verified there would have been dropped).
% Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the verbatim span
% that states it:
%
%   static -> at_rest
%     https://testbook.com/physics/types-of-friction
%     (Testbook — "Types of Friction" teaching summary)
%     "It is the type of frictional force which acts between the surfaces of two
%      objects when they are kept at rest about each other."
%
%   sliding -> sliding_motion
%     https://testbook.com/physics/types-of-friction
%     (Testbook — "Types of Friction" teaching summary)
%     "Sliding friction can be defined as the friction which is generated
%      between the surfaces of any two objects when they are in a sliding motion
%      against each other."
%
%   rolling -> spherical_object
%     https://testbook.com/physics/types-of-friction
%     (Testbook — "Types of Friction" teaching summary)
%     "It can be determined as the friction force which prevents the motion of
%      any spherical object like a wheel or a ball, etc."
%
%   fluid -> fluid_layers
%     https://testbook.com/physics/types-of-friction
%     (Testbook — "Types of Friction" teaching summary)
%     "Fluid friction is the type of friction that exists between the layers of
%      fluid when they are in relative motion with each other."
%
% (The static and sliding rows are additionally corroborated by a primary U.S.
% government source — the NASA "Friction" NextGenSTEM educator notes,
% https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/friction-cc-final-9-24-24-508.pdf
% — "Static friction is a force that opposes the motion of two objects in
% contact with each other while they are motionless" and "Kinetic friction is a
% force that opposes the motion of objects in contact with each other while one
% is moving past the other." That NASA note only covers static and kinetic, so
% it corroborates two rows but cannot ground rolling or fluid; the table's
% canonical per-row source is therefore the single Testbook page that states all
% four cleanly, kept uniform so the trust tier is honest across every row.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the Testbook statement that fixes
% the first row (static -> at_rest). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each context remains independently
% checkable. All four rows come from the same Testbook "Types of Friction"
% teaching summary — a secondary teaching resource, not a primary government or
% university source — so `trust consensus`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table friction_context {
    columns friction, context

    row (static, at_rest)
    row (sliding, sliding_motion)
    row (rolling, spherical_object)
    row (fluid, fluid_layers)

    source "It is the type of frictional force which acts between the surfaces of two objects when they are kept at rest about each other."
    locator "https://testbook.com/physics/types-of-friction"
    trust consensus
}
